#!/usr/bin/python3
#
# gce-gcs-relay --- relay requests from GCS to the LTM or KCS server
#
# Normally the gce-xfstests command sends requests to the LTM and KCS
# servers by connecting to the https port of the VM where the server is
# running.  This daemon, which runs on that VM, provides an alternative
# for when that isn't possible (for example, because of firewall rules,
# or because the VM has no external IP address): the request is placed
# in the gce-xfstests GCS bucket, this daemon passes it to the server
# using the loopback interface, and puts the server's response into
# the bucket.  The client side of this is run-fstests/util/gce-gcs-curl;
# see the "GCS relay" section of Documentation/gce-xfstests.md.
#
# The protocol is as follows.  The objects used all live under
#
#     gs://$GS_BUCKET/gcs-relay/<instance name>-<project>/
#
# (qualifying the instance name with the GCE project ID so that
# multiple GCE projects can safely share a single GCS bucket).
#
# 1) The client picks a unique request id, and uploads the request as
#    req/<id>.json.  This is a JSON object with the following members:
#
#        "version"   The protocol version; must be 1
#        "method"    The HTTP method (e.g., "POST")
#        "path"      The request target (e.g., "/login")
#        "headers"   The request headers, as a list of [name, value] lists
#        "body"      (optional) The request body, base64-encoded
#        "timeout"   How long (in seconds) the client is going to wait
#                    for the response
#
# 2) Cloud Storage publishes an OBJECT_FINALIZE notification (with
#    payload format NONE) to the project's gce-xfstests-gcs-relay
#    Pub/Sub topic.  This daemon long-polls its per-VM filtered pull
#    subscription (<instance>-gcs-relay by default) over the Pub/Sub
#    REST API, acknowledges notifications on receipt, and treats each
#    notification as a hint naming one req/<id>.json object and
#    generation.  While no requests are arriving, the daemon makes no
#    Cloud Storage API calls.
#
# 3) The daemon downloads the request, and then deletes it.  The
#    request is only acted upon if it was this daemon which managed to
#    delete it; this guarantees that a request is passed to the server
#    at most once (even if Pub/Sub delivers a notification more than
#    once, or another scan runs concurrently).  A request which is
#    older than its timeout is deleted without being acted upon, since
#    the client will have given up on it, and will have told the user
#    so.
#
# 4) The daemon sends the request to the server, and uploads the
#    response as rsp/<id>.json.  Up to --max-workers requests (8 by
#    default) are handled in parallel, so a slow request does not hold
#    up other requests arriving at the same time.  The response is a
#    JSON object with the following members:
#
#        "version"   The protocol version (1)
#        "status"    The HTTP status code
#        "reason"    The HTTP reason phrase
#        "headers"   The response headers, as a list of [name, value] lists
#        "body"      The response body, base64-encoded
#
#    or, if no response could be obtained from the server:
#
#        "version"   The protocol version (1)
#        "error"     A description of what went wrong
#        "kind"      "connect" if the server could not be reached,
#                    "timeout" if it did not answer in time,
#                    "request" if the request was malformed,
#                    or "transport" for anything else
#
# 5) The client, which has been polling for the response, downloads
#    and deletes it.  Any responses which get left behind are deleted
#    by the daemon after five minutes.
#
# The daemon only lists the bucket on startup (to adopt any requests
# uploaded before it was running), after --response-ttl seconds to
# clean up unclaimed responses, while saturated (more requests than
# the in-flight queue limit), after recovering from a Pub/Sub error,
# and when invoked with --once.
#
# Access to Cloud Storage and Pub/Sub is authenticated using the VM's
# service account.  The relay does not add to or replace the server's
# own authentication; it passes requests along as they are, and the
# server checks the session cookie or password in each request as it
# normally does.  Note that this means that requests and responses,
# which can contain the LTM password or a session cookie, are briefly
# stored in the bucket.  Anyone who can read the bucket can already
# read the password (gs://$GS_BUCKET/ltm-pass), so this does not make
# things any worse.
#
# Nothing but the Python standard library is used, so that there are no
# additional packages which need to be installed in the test appliance.

